00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.060
And now it's 22 years that I've been here.
现在我在这里已经22年了。

335
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.480
We started working on the show, I think, like right after July 4th weekend, maybe, the
我们开始为这部剧工作，我想，大概就在独立日周末之后吧，也许吧，

336
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:41.780
writers met for the first time.
编剧们第一次开了会。

337
00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:45.040
And that is inconceivable to me.
这让我觉得不可思议。

338
00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:47.940
I mean, it feels like both forever ago and also yesterday.
我是说，感觉既像是好久以前，又像是昨天。

339
00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:49.280
Well, listen.
好吧，听着。

340
00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:55.140
The last time you came on the show, we talked a lot about, like, how you got your job on the
你上次来节目时，我们聊了很多关于你是怎么得到这份工作的，

341
00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.740
show and how you became Moe's and all those things.
你是怎么成为Moe's的以及诸如此类的事情。

342
00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.620
But we didn't get a chance to dig into some of the specific episodes that you wrote.
但我们没机会深入探讨你写的一些具体的剧集。

343
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:03.100
Great.
太好了。

344
00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:03.740
Let's do it.
我们开始吧。

345
00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.060
So we were thinking maybe we could talk about a few.
所以我们在想，也许我们可以聊其中几集。

346
00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.120
So speaking of the early days, the Alliance, it's season one of The Office.
说到早期，也就是《同盟》那一集，那是《办公室》的第一季。

347
00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:16.280
And you guys are trying to establish what is the American version of this.
你们当时在努力确立美版到底该是什么样。

348
00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:16.620
Right.
对的。

349
00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:17.960
How do we make it our own thing?
我们如何才能把它变成我们自己的东西？

350
00:12:18.760 --> 00:12:20.500
Can you take me back to that time?
你能带我回到那个时候吗？

351
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:21.120
Yeah.
没错。

352
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:26.720
So that first season, it was just me, Mindy, and BJ were the only full-time writers.
所以第一季的时候，全职编剧只有我、明迪和 BJ 三个人。

353
00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.880
And then the late Lester Lewis, RIP, was a consultant.
然后已故的莱斯特·刘易斯（愿他安息）担任顾问。

354
00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.260
And Larry Wilmore was a consultant.
拉里·威尔莫尔担任顾问。

355
00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:33.940
And Paul Lieberstein was a consultant.
保罗·利伯斯坦担任顾问。

356
00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.660
And it was a very small crew.
当时团队非常小。

357
00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:45.520
And in classic Greg Daniels fashion, we came up with 270 episode ideas to make five episodes.
按格雷格·丹尼尔斯的经典作风，我们想出了 270 个剧集点子，最后拍了五集。

358
00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:51.800
And I want to say the Alliance, one of the things that is going on in the first season
我想说，《联盟》这一集，英国版《办公室》第一季中发生的事情之一

359
00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.160
of The British Office is the threat of downsizing and the threat of consolidation and a branch
就是裁员的威胁、整合的威胁、分公司

360
00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:58.260
closing and that sort of stuff.
关闭诸如此类的事情。

361
00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:59.560
It sort of runs in the background.
这有点像是在背景中进行的。

362
00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.820
And Greg wanted to maintain that because it was and is such a part of corporate life that
格雷格希望保留这一点，因为这是——而且一直是——企业生活的一部分，

363
00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:06.620
you're always like, is someone going to buy our company and are we all going to lose our
你总是会想，有没有人要收购我们公司，我们是不是都要失业了？

364
00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:06.980
jobs?
工作？

365
00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:12.680
And so he wanted that theme to run through the season in a low-grade way.
所以他希望这个主题能以一种温和的方式贯穿整个季度。

366
00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:19.680
And the simple nugget of the idea was that in this kind of vague threat of something bad
这个点子的核心很简单，就是在办公室面临某种不好的事情的模糊威胁下，

367
00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:25.280
happening in The Office, that because Survivor was such a big deal at the time, like people
因为当时《幸存者》太火了，人们都忘了，比如《幸存者》是在 2000 年左右开播的。

368
00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.540
forget, like Survivor had debuted, I think, in the year 2000 or something.
或者类似的时间。

369
00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.020
And around 2004, it was like the biggest deal.
到了 2004 年左右，它简直火得一塌糊涂。

370
00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.240
It still is a huge deal, but it was the biggest deal.
现在它依然很火，但当时是最火的。

371
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:39.540
And I want to say it was BJ, my memory might be failing me, who pitched the idea of just
我想是 BJ 提出的这个想法——也许我记错了——

372
00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.000
Dwight asking Jim to form an alliance with him, Survivor style.
他提出让德怀特模仿《幸存者》的风格，邀请吉姆和他结盟。

373
00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.300
And we liked that idea.
我们很喜欢这个主意。

374
00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.800
Obviously, like Jim and Dwight, famous antagonists.
显然，吉姆和德怀特是一对著名的死对头。

375
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:53.000
But the breakthrough was that someone pitched, I'd like to take credit for it, but I don't
但突破点在于有人提议——我很想把这功劳揽下来，但我不确定自己能不能——吉姆的回答是，没问题，我愿意。

376
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.360
know if I can, that Jim's response was, absolutely, I do.
绝对愿意。

377
00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.300
Like instantly.
几乎是瞬间的事。

378
00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.960
I was going to ask you about that because that's like such a famous couplet that comes
我要问你这个，因为这实在是太经典了，以后还会出现。

379
00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:02.600
back later.
还会回来。

380
00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:04.660
It was absolutely, I do.
那就是“当然，我愿意”。

381
00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:10.760
And then the talking head that comes after it is, anytime Dwight asks me to do anything,
紧接着的单人访谈镜头是，只要德怀特让我做任何事，

382
00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:11.920
my response is absolutely.
我的回答都是“当然”。

383
00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:20.220
Because Jim's sole goal in life in that moment is to like enjoy this mundane business environment
因为吉姆在那一刻唯一的Живце人生目标就是以他能找到的任何方式去享受这个单调的商务环境。

384
00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.580
in any way he possibly can.
用尽一切可能的方式。

385
00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:22.980
Just escape the boredom.
只为了逃避无聊。

386
00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:23.920
Just escape the boredom.
只为了逃避无聊。

387
00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.460
And it's one of the great lessons of writing that I've learned a hundred thousand times,
这是我历经千百次才学到的编剧伟大教训之一，

388
00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:31.020
which it's a classic improv thing, right?
这其实就是即兴表演的经典法则，对吧？

389
00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:32.920
It's like, yes, and it like, just say yes.
就像是“好，而且……”，直接说好就行。

390
00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.400
If you say no, the story ends.
如果你说不，故事就结束了。

391
00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:35.780
If you say yes, you get a story.
如果你说好，故事就展开了。

392
00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:40.900
So as soon as we came up with the idea that Dwight makes an absurd request of Jim and Jim's
所以当我们想到德怀特对吉姆提出一个荒谬的要求，而吉姆

393
00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:44.860
immediate response is yes, then, okay, we're off and running.
的即时反应是“好”时，好吧，我们就此文思泉涌了。

394
00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.140
So that was the like simple nugget of the story.
所以这就是故事的简单核心。

395
00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:55.720
And then it became like, how does Jim enjoy like torturing Dwight and enjoying his life
然后就演变成：吉姆如何在枯燥的一天中享受折磨德怀特、享受生活的乐趣，

396
00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.960
in this boring day and sharing it with Pam, of course.
当然，还要和帕姆分享这一切。

397
00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:04.980
My favorite scene that came out of it is the one that's sort of a spy scene from up top,
我最喜欢的衍生场景是那个类似间谍视角的镜头，从上往下俯视

398
00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:06.780
looking down at them in the parking lot.
停车场里的他们。

399
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.360
And they're standing outside.
他们正站在外面。

400
00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.140
And it was, as it always was in the valley, 111 degrees.
而且，就像谷地里常有的那样，气温高达 111 华氏度。

401
00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.740
But it was supposed to be like November in Scranton.
但当时本该像是斯克兰顿的十一月。

402
00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:21.500
And so the two of them just are grabbing their arms in the classic pose of person.
然后他们两个就以人类的标准姿势抓着彼此的手臂。

403
00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:22.540
I'm cold.
我很冷。

404
00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:27.280
Which we would then do 50 more times over the course of the first couple seasons.
在头几季的过程中，我们后来又重复了五十多次。

405
00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:33.500
And having that like private kind of survivor-like conversation about like, you know, how to play
进行那种私密的、类似《幸存者》式的对话，探讨诸如

406
00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.480
things and what they've learned and all that stuff.
如何处理事情、他们学到了什么之类的内容。

407
00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:36.800
So that was it.
所以就是这样。

408
00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.120
It was what made that show so special to me was like, that's what you needed for a story.
这也是那部剧对我而言如此特别的原因，这就是讲故事所需要的东西。

409
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.500
You didn't need a gigantic, huge plot.
你不需要宏大无比的情节。

410
00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:49.260
You needed one of the ridiculous characters to make a request of a sane character and have
你只需要其中一个荒谬的角色向一个理智的角色提出要求，并让

411
00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.620
the sane character decide to go along with it for the day.
那个理智的角色决定在这一天里顺着他胡闹。

412
00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:52.440
And that was it.
事情就是这样。

413
00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:55.240
That's a really great way to look at The Office now.
这真的是看待《办公室》的一个绝佳视角。

414
00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:59.340
Like the next time I watch an episode, I'll be like, okay, who's the ridiculous character
就像下次我看剧集时，我会想，好吧，这次是谁这个荒谬的角色

415
00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.180
asking the sane character to do something?
要求那个理智的角色去做某事？

416
00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:01.300
That's all it is.
这就是全部内容了。

417
00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:01.660
Yeah.
是啊。

418
00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:06.700
And it's usually Michael is the ridiculous one asking Pam or Jim or someone to do something,
通常都是迈克尔这个荒谬的人要求帕姆、吉姆或其他人去做某事，

419
00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.660
go to Hooters or whatever it was.
比如去猫头鹰餐厅或者诸如此类的事情。

420
00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:14.740
It was either a sane person doing something sane and watching the lunatics respond or vice
要么是一个理智的人做着理智的事，然后看着这群疯子作出反应，反之亦然，

421
00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:18.940
versa, like a lunatic doing something lunatic and then the sane person's responding.
就像一个疯子做着疯狂的事，然后理智的人做出反应。

422
00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.160
You also wrote Valentine's Day.
你还写了《情人节》那一集。

423
00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:22.780
Yeah.
是的。

424
00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:26.100
And this puts the character of Michael Scott in New York City.
这把迈克尔·斯科特这个角色放到了纽约市。

425
00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:26.700
Yes.
是的。

426
00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.420
There are so many funny moments.
有太多搞笑的时刻了。

427
00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.400
What was it like to do that?
那样做是什么感觉？

428
00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:32.980
Like you're from the East CoaSt.
就像你是东海岸出身一样。

429
00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:33.300
Yeah.
是的。

430
00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:36.900
So seeing New York City through Michael Scott's eyes.
所以是通过迈克尔·斯科特的眼睛来观察纽约市。

431
00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:38.780
So that was season two.
所以那是第二季。

432
00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:39.520
Season two.
第二季。

433
00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:47.500
And it was the beginning of Greg wanting to expand the world of the show occasionally,
那是格雷格开始想要偶尔、罕见地扩展剧集世界的开端，

434
00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:54.340
rarely, but saying like we can break out a little bit of the rule we had stuck to,
他说，我们可以稍微打破一下我们一直坚守的规则，

435
00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:58.040
which is every episode happens in one day and every episode happens inside the walls of
也就是每一集都在一天内发生，并且每一集都发生在

436
00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.120
the office or on the grounds of Dunder Mifflin.
办公室的墙内或者邓德米夫林的土地上。

437
00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.760
And so we had this idea that he would go to New York.
所以我们有了这个构想，让他去一趟纽约。

438
00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.740
There were two really important things that I think came out of that episode.
我认为那一集里有两件非常重要的事情诞生了。

439
00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:10.940
The first was like, let's take a skeleton crew to the city, walk around with Steve Carell
第一件就像是，让我们带一个精简的摄制组去城里，和史蒂夫·卡瑞尔一起四处走走

440
00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:13.140
and just do a bunch of jokes.
然后拍一堆笑话。

441
00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:15.940
In the room, I remember we called it Michael Scott's New York.
在编剧室里，我记得我们把它叫做“迈克尔·斯科特的纽约”。

442
00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:20.660
That's how he thought of it was like, because his whole thing is I'm an expert on everything.
他是这么看待它的，因为他的全部态度就是“我是个万事通”。

443
00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:21.080
Yes.
没错。

444
00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:25.280
And so we thought it would be really fun to put him in Manhattan, a place where like
所以我们觉得把他放到曼哈顿会非常有趣，那是个你必须

445
00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:29.900
you've got to, you know, spend a lot of time in Manhattan to be an expert on it.
你知道的，在曼哈顿花大量时间才能成为行家的地方。

446
00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:30.900
And he is not.
而他显然不是。

447
00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:36.140
So the joke I wrote, that's probably the most famous joke of that episode is the one where
所以我写的那个笑话，可能也是那一集里最著名的笑话，就是他

448
00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.240
he talks about taking the camera crew to his favorite pizza place.
谈到带着摄制组去他最喜欢的披萨店的那段。

449
00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:39.660
Yes.
是的。

450
00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:40.740
And it's a Sparrow.
而那是一家斯帕罗披萨店。

451
00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:41.260
Yeah.
是啊。

452
00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:43.780
It's the one that comes back to me the most still to this day.
直到今天，它依然是我脑海中最常浮现的一个。

453
00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:51.620
I mean, people post online pictures of themselves in front of Sparrow and saying, you know.
我的意思是，人们会在网上发自己站在斯帕罗餐厅前的照片，然后说，你懂的。

454
00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:51.920
Yeah.
对。

455
00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:53.100
Got to get a New York slice.
得来一块纽约披萨。

456
00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:53.380
Yeah.
对。

457
00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:53.720
Yeah.
是啊。

458
00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.020
That episode is largely about him and Jan.
那一集主要讲的是他和简。

459
00:17:57.340 --> 00:17:57.620
Yes.
没错。

460
00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:02.340
And him believing that he has a relationship with Jan that he does not actually have.
还有他坚信自己和简有一种实际上并不存在的恋爱关系。

461
00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:06.920
But this was the other really important thing about that episode from a like a world building
但从世界观构建的角度来看，

462
00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:13.660
point of view was a question that had been asked many times of the show by critics and
那一集的另一个极其重要的意义在于，剧评人、

463
00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:18.360
fans and all sorts of people was, how did this dummy get this job?
粉丝以及形形色色的人曾经多次向剧组提出过一个问题：这家伙是怎么得到这份工作的？

464
00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:18.680
Right.
对吧。

465
00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:20.200
How did Michael Scott get this job?
迈克尔·斯科特是怎么得到这份工作的？

466
00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.120
And Greg had thought it out very carefully.
格雷格对此进行了非常仔细的思考。

467
00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:26.420
It was a living example of what's called in corporate structure, the Peter Principle,
这正是企业架构中所称的“彼得原理”的生动例证，

468
00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:26.620
right?
对吧？

469
00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:26.960
Yes.
是的。

470
00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:28.440
We know the Peter Principle.
我们都知道彼得原理。

471
00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:29.640
We've discussed, right?
我们讨论过，对吧？

472
00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:33.920
People who do a good job at their job are then promoted to a position in which their skills
在工作中表现出色的人会被晋升到他们原有的技能

473
00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.700
have run out and are no longer applicable or not qualified.
不再适用或不再具备资格的岗位上。

474
00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:42.400
But it was still a question of like, okay, even given that this guy has done a lot of
但仍然存在这样一个问题，好吧，即便这家伙做过很多

475
00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.980
ridiculous stuff, so how do we justify it?
荒唐的事，我们该如何合理解释呢？

